WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:08.980
Greetings, and welcome to today's webinar,
a new way to prepare genome submissions for

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:09.980
GenBank.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.270
My name is Colleen Bollin.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:16.850
In addition to being the technical program
manager now , in the past I previously worked

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:21.890
on the development team for Sequin , and later
the development team for Genome Workbench.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:28.390
Today I will be demonstrating how to use the
editing package in Genome Workbench to prepare

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.980
genome submissions for a single organism in
GenBank.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.570
So, what is Genome Workbench?

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:40.960
It is a GUI application that runs on Windows,
Linux, MacOS.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:47.040
And provides a rich set of tools for creating
and viewing phylogenetic trees, alignments,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.080
tabular data and more.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.350
You can use it with public data from NCBI
or elsewhere or with your own private data

00:00:54.350 --> 00:01:00.420
. You can also use it to prepare your own
data for submission to GenBank.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.780
Peter is going to launch a poll questions.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.080
If you can take a minute to answer that.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.900
Okay, so everybody should be seeing this polling
question.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.540
It is how familiar are you with Genome Workbench.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.311
Go ahead and select all of the ones that apply.

00:01:13.311 --> 00:01:17.680
I will go ahead and close that polling question
now.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.790
And you should be seeing the results.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.530
So, a wide distribution of experience here.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.780
Some of you have never heard of it.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.770
Many of you have heard of it but have never
used it.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.470
Some others have some experience with it.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.840
That is a wide distribution of experience
with this.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:40.970
I will hide it, and we should be able to see
your sides again.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:42.220
Okay, great.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:47.550
As I mentioned before, we have a slide for
how you can download your own copy of Genome

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.550
Workbench.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:49.550
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/tools/gbench/downloads/

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.270
So, follow the instructions on the side.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.670
Please do check out our website for manuals
and tutorials.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.240
We have lots.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.760
Our customer outreach people are mad at me
because I am using the tiny font size just

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.270
to get the tutorials, not even the manuals,
all on one page.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.369
There is lots of good stuff.

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:11.870
We have a YouTube channel.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:15.689
Lots to learn there.

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:20.219
Genome Workbench has many valuable tools for
modifying your submission and doing other

00:02:20.219 --> 00:02:24.810
kinds of analysis , and we won't be able to
cover all of them today.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:30.310
These are some of the ones from editing that
we won't get to today, but they are documented

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:35.129
on the website that I just mentioned, and
in the tutorials and manuals.

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:39.950
So, I am going to switch over to the live
demo.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:46.150
The first thing we need to do when you first
download Genome Workbench, you need to enable

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.669
the editing package.

00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:52.219
To do that, you launch Genome Workbench , and
you go to the tools menu.

00:02:52.219 --> 00:02:54.540
You select packages.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.359
You pick the sequence editing package.

00:02:57.359 --> 00:03:00.189
You click enable, and you click okay.

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:04.989
It is going to say changes will take effect
when you restart Genome Workbench, so let's

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:07.120
do that now.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:18.059
Peter, while we are waiting for Genome Workbench
to start up, could you start the second polling

00:03:18.059 --> 00:03:19.059
question?

00:03:19.059 --> 00:03:20.849
Yes I will do that.

00:03:20.849 --> 00:03:21.980
Let me see.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:22.980
Okay.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.530
Okay, I will launch the pole.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.879
The question here is, which tools have you
used to prepare submissions to GenBank . Please

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:29.879
answer.

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:31.700
We will let that run for maybe 30 seconds
to give everybody a chance to vote.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.269
And you know, one of the options here is other.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:38.809
If you are using something else, I don't know
what that might be, but you can put that in

00:03:38.809 --> 00:03:40.389
the questions pod if you want.

00:03:40.389 --> 00:03:42.659
Okay, let's give it about 5 more seconds.

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:43.659
Okay.

00:03:43.659 --> 00:03:44.659
I am going to close the poll now.

00:03:44.659 --> 00:03:45.659
I am sharing it.

00:03:45.659 --> 00:03:46.831
I see a lot of fans of Sequin.

00:03:46.831 --> 00:03:51.889
Sequin is the biggest one there . Then BankIt
then tbl2asn.

00:03:51.889 --> 00:03:58.150
Some people have used Genome Workbench before
to do this and then there are some others

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.359
in there as well.

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:00.359
Great.

00:04:00.359 --> 00:04:04.120
So now we are back in Genome Workbench and
one of the things you should notice that is

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.529
different is that we now have a submission
menu.

00:04:07.529 --> 00:04:12.400
That was not there before because we had not
enabled the editing package.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.769
At the top of the submission menu is the Genome
Submission Wizard.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:24.930
Because I did not have a sequence record open,
it is prompting me to open one now.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.479
If I already had one open, it would use the
one I already had open.

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:29.479
If I already had one open, it would use that.

00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:31.560
I am just going to choose this file here.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.449
For the purposes of this demonstration, I
am going to use a draft version of a whole

00:04:36.449 --> 00:04:42.030
genome shotgun submission that was prepared
using tbl2asn a commandline tool available

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:47.389
on the NCBI ftp site that many of you are
already familiar with.

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:54.000
I could also have used a file that was prepared
by NCBI's prokaryotic genome annotation pipeline,

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.039
also known as PGAP, or with other tools that
generate valid ASN 1.

00:04:59.039 --> 00:05:02.090
Or I could have used a file with FASTA sequences.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:07.030
So, now that I have the file opened, you will
notice two things.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:11.689
The first is that the wizard has pulled in
the submitter name and email address that

00:05:11.689 --> 00:05:14.090
I provided in the ASN 1 file.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:18.669
We are going to see the other information
already in the file as we go through the other

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:19.669
tabs.

00:05:19.669 --> 00:05:25.699
The wizard will prompt me for required information
that could not get out of the file that we

00:05:25.699 --> 00:05:26.699
imported.

00:05:26.699 --> 00:05:32.439
The second thing you should notice is that
the wizard automatically opened up a Flat

00:05:32.439 --> 00:05:34.160
File View of my sequences.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:39.960
I get a preview of how my sequences would
look on the GenBank website.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:47.080
The information in the flat file will be updated
as I move between tabs and I make changes.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:51.909
Note that this is different from Sequin , and
that with Sequin you always had to start with

00:05:51.909 --> 00:05:58.080
FASTA files and then enter the data manually
or import a template.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:04.819
You can still import and export templates
from the wizard at any time using the import

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:13.229
from template button here on the bottom or
the export to template button on the right.

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:15.810
That should be familiar to you, hopefully.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:21.669
So, here we have the affiliation sub tab and
you can see the asterisks to tell you which

00:06:21.669 --> 00:06:24.159
fields are required.

00:06:24.159 --> 00:06:28.090
You can also see the information that was
imported from my ASN 1 file.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:33.409
You should also notice is back and continue
buttons at the bottom left and bottom right

00:06:33.409 --> 00:06:35.099
of the dialogue.

00:06:35.099 --> 00:06:40.050
You can use those to move through the tabs,
or you can skip around using the tab controls

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.729
up at the top.

00:06:42.729 --> 00:06:50.280
So, on the General tab, I can enter the bioproject
and biosample IDs and indicate when the data

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:51.919
will be made public.

00:06:51.919 --> 00:06:58.419
You do not need to add IDs here because you
will have an opportunity to enter them when

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:01.379
you upload the file to the submission portal.

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:07.439
You do need to preregister a bioproject and
a biosample if you are submitting with annotation,

00:07:07.439 --> 00:07:13.879
but otherwise the portal will help you create
those at the time that you upload your file.

00:07:13.879 --> 00:07:24.060
You can create bioproject and BioSample successions
in the pages . And Internet search for submission

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:30.300
BioProject will also find the correct page
for you.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:35.940
Moving on, we have assembly and sequence information.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:39.889
You have the option of providing the assembly
and sequencing information here, or in the

00:07:39.889 --> 00:07:42.689
web forms where you submit your sequences
later.

00:07:42.689 --> 00:07:48.080
If you provide them here, you can see how
they will look in the flat file, and you won't

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.889
have to reenter them.

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:55.819
Let's move on to the organism info pay.

00:07:55.819 --> 00:07:59.740
Here this information was extracted from the
ASN 1 file that I imported.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:06.629
I do not need to extract it again or worry
that it will not match what I already entered.

00:08:06.629 --> 00:08:11.750
You can also see there are double asterisks
after strain, isolate, cultivar, and breed.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:19.159
You need to provide information for at least
one of these fields.

00:08:19.159 --> 00:08:24.050
On the additional qualifiers page, the strain
information that we found in the imported

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:25.710
file is also here.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:31.970
You can use this page to add more information,
like the collection date, or correct information

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:33.900
that is already in the file.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:38.160
You also have an opportunity to provide this
information when you create your BioProject

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.750
and BioSample accessions.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:45.140
The data will be copied to your submission
during the upload process so you will not

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:50.960
need to do it twice.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:56.650
This is a draft genome, so we do not have
individual sequences to represent the entire

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.650
chromosome or organelles.

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:06.710
In that case, we can leave these tabs blank
and move on to annotation.

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:12.230
So, the annotation tab.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:17.110
I am really excited about this tab, because
it has some great new features that were not

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.440
in Sequin.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:24.760
For example , the existing features that I
already created in my file are displayed here

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:28.380
using the NCBI five column feature table format.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:36.270
I could also use this import from feature
table button at the upper left-hand corner

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:44.030
of the dialogue to import a file with more
features, and I can do that from the five

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:48.190
column feature table format, or GFF3.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:53.870
We have had a lot of requests for GFF3 and
we are excited to be able to offer it to you

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:55.040
now.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:02.890
I wanted to point out that some of the fields
that you see here, like translation table,

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:07.440
those were automatically calculated for you
when the annotation was imported.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.530
You do not need to provide them yourself.

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:14.650
So, if you need more information about the
five column feature table, there is a link

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:18.930
to that in the notes for this slide deck.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:30.580
Moving on, on the sequence authors tab, I
would list the name of the researchers responsible

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.520
for sequencing data.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:37.710
Note that this list does not have to include
the contact person for the submission that

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:42.460
was on the first tab.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.810
But it could.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:52.540
Now, on the publication sub tab, I am going
to enter information about the publication

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:58.130
that is associated with the submission, including
the title or working title for unpublished

00:10:58.130 --> 00:10:59.430
articles.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:03.860
The publication authors and the sequence authors
can be different.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:09.700
If you would like them to be the same, you
can select the same as sequence authors radio

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:13.390
button in the center of the dialogue.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:18.740
File I was working with had different authors,
so the radio button was automatically set

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:24.690
to the other option which is specify new authors.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:34.540
So, the validation tab has two sub tabs, validate
and submitter report.

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:40.170
These two tabs will show you the kinds of
errors and warnings that GenBank indexers

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:43.770
would look at when reviewing your submission.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:48.870
So , if you can correct the problems ahead
of time, your submission can be processed

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:51.000
much faster.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.490
Note that you do need to manually trigger
each of these reports after making changes

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.790
or coming to the tab for the first time.

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:03.080
Examining the record can take a minute or
two so we do not do it automatically after

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:06.490
each update.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:11.290
For both of these reports, you can single
click on the item that the error or warning

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:15.950
refers to, and the submission wizard will
automatically scroll to the portion of the

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:17.850
flat file that shows that item.

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:21.980
If you double-click on the item, the editor
for that item will be launched.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:23.820
We will show that later.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:31.270
Four help interpreting and fixing problems,
there is a link to a help page in the note

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:32.270
for this slide deck.

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.670
I will validate the record.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:40.390
So, a common problem that we see in submissions
is stop and protein.

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:41.390
That is the third errorstop and protein.

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:44.000
That is the third error that we see here.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.070
There could be many reasons for this error.

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:50.300
One is that the wrong translation frame was
selected for the coding region.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:56.600
If we single click on this error, you will
note that the flat file has automatically

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:58.820
scrolled to that feature.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:05.000
We are looking at it and we can see, okay,
this coding region starts at the five prime

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.170
end of the sequence, so it is possible that
this is a partial coding region and we just

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:13.650
need to change the frame.

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:19.120
So, quickly right now, I would like to take
a look at the flat file.

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:25.600
You will see a pen and an X icon that you
can use, the pen will allow you to add a coding

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:29.070
region and the X will allow you to delete
the coding region.

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:35.000
These will only be visible when you have the
editing package enabled.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:40.290
I can either use the pen icon to launch the
editor here or I can double-click on it from

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:41.350
the validator.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:45.670
I'm going to double-click here and we will
launch the editor for the coding region.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:51.970
So, in the editor, I can experiment with changing
the reading frame using this reading frame

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:55.450
control here, and then using the re-translate
button.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:14:00.510
We will change it to two, and I see that I
still have stop codons in this translation

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.160
box.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:08.360
Let's try changing it to three, and re-translating,
and look, we have a translation that has a

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.230
stop codon where the stop codon should be.

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:13.290
Excellent.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:15.940
It is clearly meant to be frame three.

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:23.510
Now, I would like you to notice that I have
the Update Gene span box checked by default,

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:28.990
which means that if I make changes to the
location of the coding region, the gene location

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:32.750
will get changed at the same time so they
will stay this dame.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:39.070
This is important because we also want to
mark this coding region as five prime partial.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:45.550
To do that, we will click on the Location
in the left-hand panel and we will just check

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:47.190
the five prime box.

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:48.190
That is all.

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:52.680
Now we will click on okay to finish editing.

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:57.110
And you can see that the flat file is refreshing
itself.

00:14:57.110 --> 00:15:01.640
Now I am going to validate the record again.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:07.910
I could just move on to a different error
to work on something else before re-validating,

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:11.150
but sometimes when you make one change, it
actually fixes possible problems.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:13.400
Let's just revalidate this record.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:18.680
So, now I want to look at the last two validator
errors.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.520
Terminal Ns, and the high N 5% prime.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:30.450
They both refer to the same sequence.

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:37.310
You can see over here, in the sequence column,
and I can single click on it will navigate

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:41.010
to the contig003 sequence for me in the flat
file.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:47.690
So, you have two ways to fix this kind of
an error.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:57.450
One of those as you can go to the submissions,
sequences, trim terminal Ns menu item and

00:15:57.450 --> 00:15:59.460
click on.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:06.040
This will remove them from the 5 prime and
3 prime ends of all of your sequences, and

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:10.460
adjust the annotation automatically for you
so that the features still cover the same

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:15.980
nucleotides, and it will trim features that
overlap the Ns , and re-translate the coding

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:20.830
regions that were trimmed and give you a nice
report.

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:23.730
However, I want to show you a different way
to do this.

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:29.150
Am going to use the undo method over here
in the edit menu, and if you are familiar

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:34.200
with Sequin, you will remember that we had
no undo . This is a great thing we are really

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:35.910
excited about.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:43.550
So instead I am going to go submission, sequences,
edit sequence, whips.

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:46.130
Here we go.

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:49.920
We are going to pick contig003.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.590
Sometimes when you undo something that changes
the focus, but that just means have to go

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:56.170
back and select your sequence again.

00:16:56.170 --> 00:17:03.111
Anyways, let's go to submission, sequences,
edit sequences, and here we see the sequence

00:17:03.111 --> 00:17:04.111
editor.

00:17:04.111 --> 00:17:09.030
So, this is also a nifty thing that I am really
proud of.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:13.530
One of the things you can do with the sequence
editor is you can just use your mouse to drag

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:19.150
and highlight the nucleotides that you want
to delete, and then you can hit the delete

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:22.260
key, and they are gone.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:28.720
They are not really gone until I hit the commit
button, which I will do now, so that I can

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:31.470
make other changes.

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:40.350
You could also use this to remove vector contamination
or low-quality sequence that you found using

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.310
some other tool.

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:44.740
Also, this is nice.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:51.350
If you don't want to select a large sequence
with your mouse, or you want to be very precise

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:57.780
about which nucleotides you are selecting,
you can just type in the locations that you

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:00.600
want in the select box and click on select.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:04.760
You will see that they are highlighted, which
is nifty.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:12.070
One of the things you can do is you can use
the sequence editing dialog to add new features.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:18.970
If you have selected a location, and then
you go to your features menu, and pull up

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:24.150
your feature, the location is going to be
your selection.

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:28.250
You can just, you can do it that way.

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:33.060
Another thing you can do with the sequence
editing tool is, here we can see the new feature

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:38.800
I just created, you can use the show menu
to display reading frames.

00:18:38.800 --> 00:18:45.140
I can show all of my positive direction reading
frames, and I can also show the complement.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:49.810
There are some other things that you can find
out about in the show menu.

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:50.810
Check out our manual.

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:52.040
It is really neat.

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:56.170
We are just going to close this, and now you
can see we have two misc features.

00:18:56.170 --> 00:19:02.090
I'm going to go back to the submission wizard
just hanging out in the background that I'm

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:05.320
going to move on to the submitter report.

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:08.110
I will refresh that so we can get the results.

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:13.520
So, the submitter report shows problems with
the submission that might involve multiple

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:20.840
objects, or situations where it is unclear
which of multiple items is incorrect, or also

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:26.020
patterns that may or may not indicate a problem
with the record, but that we would like the

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:27.540
submitter to review.

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:33.650
For example, it is possible for two different
sequences to have some of the same metadata

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:39.580
values, but often it is an indication that
they made a mistake during data entry.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:45.170
Here, let us take a look at this first item
in the report which says we have two genes

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:47.280
with duplicate locus tags.

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:53.990
Now, locus tags are required to be unique,
so we should change one of them.

00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:59.750
Which one we change is up to you, but how
are we going to find out which locus tags

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:01.200
are already in use?

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:07.810
To do that, I'm going to take another look
at a report from the submission reports menu

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:11.550
called the flat file summary.

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:16.640
This is a view that represents all of the
flat files from all of the sequences in your

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:22.190
submission, displaying the unique lines and
counting how many times they appear.

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:27.460
This was originally derived from the idea
of using the UNIX commands sort, uniq, count

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:29.160
on the flat file output.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:33.900
The report is divided into the sections that
appear on the flat file.

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:38.200
Note that this is a draft WGS submission and
it is not fully annotated.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:42.440
If it was a larger sequence that it had more
annotation, it would take a little longer

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:45.270
to generate the summary.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:51.360
We are going to move on and we are going to
look at the features.

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:57.350
Here we can see a list of all the qualifiers
that appear on any feature in our submission,

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:02.430
and we also see a list of the different kinds
of features that appear in our submission.

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:09.460
I am going to click on locus tag, and I'm
going to see that there are 4 items with locus

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:14.490
tag tst_0001 and 2 with 0002.

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:23.640
It tells me that these are where we have a
duplicate, and also that my next available

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:27.770
value is 0003.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:32.510
So I'm going to click here, tst_ooo1, and
now I have a list of all of the features wear

00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:34.840
that locus tag appears.

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:40.600
You may have noticed that the list includes
both the coding regions and the genes.

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:45.890
This is because in the flat file, the coding
region inherits the qualifiers of the gene

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:50.790
in which it is associated, even though the
locus tag and other qualifiers are actually

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:54.000
stored on the Gene feature.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:59.330
It is possible to put Gene qualifiers directly
onto your coding regions and other features

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:04.760
, which will override what was inherited from
the Gene , but this is unnecessary.

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:07.930
It may be confusing for you.

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:12.320
You will not be able to update the gene by
editing the other feature.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:18.690
So, like the submitter report and the validator,
we can launch the editor for the Gene feature

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:25.790
by double-clicking on the item in the submitter
report, or by double-clicking on the feature

00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:26.790
here.

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:29.520
I'm going to click on this second Gene and
I'm going to double-click on it.

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:34.050
I am launching the editor and I'm going to
go fix the locus tag to 3.

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:36.570
I will click on okay.

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:42.490
I am going to refresh my flat file summary,
and I will look at my locus tags.

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:51.400
Now I can see that I have 2 for 0001, 2 for
0002, and 2 for 0003.

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:58.960
We will dismiss this now and I will refresh
my submitter report, and I see that the duplicate

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:01.190
locus tax report is gone.

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:02.270
All right.

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:08.340
There is one other thing I want to show you
today, which is a graphical view.

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:12.340
So, to get there, you have two options.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:18.180
You can either go to the data in your project
view and select it and then right-click and

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:20.110
pick open new view.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:23.930
Or you can go to the view menu and click on
open new view.

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:26.650
It's going to be the same for both of them
from here.

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:31.330
I'm going to scroll down to the Sequence views
and I'm going to click on graphical sequence

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:35.350
view and then I will pick one of the sequences
I want to take a look at.

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:40.720
I can use a graphical view to look at either
the nucleotide sequences or the protein sequences,

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:46.080
but I'm going to pick contig0001 the nucleotide
sequence.

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:52.620
So, like the sequence editor, the graphical
view is a great way to see a representation

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:57.590
of the locations of the various features on
the sequence in relation to each other, but

00:23:57.590 --> 00:24:03.440
it does not wrap the sequence like the sequence
editor dies, and you can zoom in and out to

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.520
see more or less at the sequence at once.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:10.960
This is especially good when you have lots
of long sequence, when you have a long sequence

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:13.170
or lots of features.

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:18.240
Here I will highlight part of the sequence
by going to the ruler and highlighting it,

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:23.180
and then I am going to use this fifth magnifying
glass over with the tooltip that says zoom

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:26.720
to selection to, wow, zoom to selection.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:34.860
I'm going to adjust my selection by dragging
my endpoints.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:38.670
And I will zoom to selection again.

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:43.870
So, once you have gotten to a place or you
can see your features on the screen, you can

00:24:43.870 --> 00:24:48.020
have your, you can hover over it with the
mouse and it will show you a tooltip that

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:50.610
gives you a lot of information about the underlying
feature.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:56.740
The other thing you can do is you can right
click on your future and you will have edit

00:24:56.740 --> 00:25:02.140
selected objects as one of your options, among
other things, and you can bring up the same

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:09.559
editing menu that we saw from the flat file,
the flat file summary, the validator tab,

00:25:09.559 --> 00:25:11.480
and the submitter report.

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:19.090
So, when you are done making whatever changes
that you want to make with your submission,

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:22.230
there are two ways that you can save your
work.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:29.070
Now, the two best options are either to use
this lovely little finish button on the submission

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:38.280
wizard, or you can use the submission, save
submission file menu item.

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:45.220
You could also do this, which I do not recommend.

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:55.350
You could go here to, you could click here,
go to file, export data, pick ASN, select

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:59.730
the Seq submit, and then click finish and
it will save your file for you.

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:03.480
However you will notice that that was a bunch
more steps than you probably want to remember

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:04.840
or use.

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:10.000
However, you may want to use export data if
you wanted to save this in other formats like

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:11.480
the flat file or fast a.

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:14.760
So.

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:17.840
That is it for my talk for today.

