00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:14.690
Next question?

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:22.040
A couple of sort of, you know, nitty-gritty
questions about, you know, when you are doing

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:23.040
submissions.

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:27.299
How does one assert that a three prime partial
gene on one contig and five prime partial

00:29:27.299 --> 00:29:32.690
on another contig share a locus tag, that
is the same gene but with a sequence gap.

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:36.000
Can you do that?

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:37.100
Karen?

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.360
Yes, you can do that.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:47.170
What we have been suggesting people do, one
of the things that we did not go into in this

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:53.320
webinar is that the coding regions, the proteins
also have to have a protein I.D., and the

00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:58.260
protein IDs have to be unique, and generally
we recommend that you use a locus tag for

00:29:58.260 --> 00:29:59.480
your protein I.D.

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:07.600
In this case, in this question where you have
a split gene, it is sort of a hack, but what

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:14.420
we have been recommending is that, that for
the genome, one contig, will be locus tag

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:20.290
... A, and the other one will be the same
beginning string, but will be B. And then

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:26.940
the protein IDs can also be A and B. That
keeps everything unique, and, you know, we

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:34.260
have people, you know, it is a split gene,
and the other part is over here on this contig.

00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:36.799
That is what we recommend for that situation.

00:30:36.799 --> 00:30:46.049
Another possibility is, because we accept
gapped sequences as the submission, that they

00:30:46.049 --> 00:30:53.549
would actually be on the same sequence, and
the gene would span the gap.

00:30:53.549 --> 00:30:59.661
Anyway, I don't want to get into the details
of all that, but that is another option, that

00:30:59.661 --> 00:31:05.310
you give us the scaffold as the submission,
and then annotate that later.

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:07.150
Thanks, Karen.

